meta {
  name: Get Product Subpath - Relationships
  type: http
  seq: 10
}

get {
  url: {{pim_base_url_v3}}/products/6a38f614c3d65a5f868b1a06/relationships
  body: none
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{access_token}}
  Accept: application/json
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body.data: isArray
  res.body.data[0].relationship_label: eq related_products
}

docs {
  Confirmed live 2026-09-07 against "David's Dev Account".

  ## What it does
  `GET /products/{id}/relationships` -- returns
  `[{relationship_id, relationship_label, product_id}, ...]`. This account
  DOES have relationships configured (unlike the "none identified yet"
  placeholder in `config/api-testing.config.md` before this test) --
  useful fixture data for Phase 3e (`14-Relationships`) later.

  ## Shape note
  This subpath's shape is a flatter summary than the `product_relationships`
  field embedded in the full `GET /products/{id}` response, which nests
  `related_products: [{quantity, last_modified}, ...]` per relationship
  entry. Neither matches `openapi_pimv3.json`'s `relationships` field name
  at all (see `Get Product by ID.bru` and `Search Products - Filters.bru`
  for the `product_relationships` vs. `relationships` naming discrepancy).

  Also confirmed via `_fields=product_relationships` on the search
  endpoint: this same product's `product_family_id` is populated
  (`6a38f5d8c3d65a5f868b186e`) even though `num_variations: 0` -- real
  product-family fixture data exists in this account, useful for Phase 3d
  (`13-Product-Families`).
}
